      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/url": [
      {
        "@id": "https://montreal.mutek.org/en/artists/delphine-kim-theriault-de-carufel"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasDerivedFrom": [
      {
        "@id": "https://montreal.mutek.org/en/artists/delphine-kim-theriault-de-carufel"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/description": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Delphine Kim Thériault De Carufel  CA/QC — The Phantom of Freud’s Wish  The Phantom of Freud’s Wish raises a number of pertinent questions to our modern age: Do robots have a gender? What happens when these automatons start to think for themselves? How should we view this artificial consciousness evolving beyond identity and gender? This new world, anchored in the liberation of masculine and feminine identities, births a desire to come out of one’s shell and to reconsider binary beings, all through the perspective of an androgynous robot who speaks of art."
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/name": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Delphine Kim Thériault De Carufel"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "_:g16170600",
    "@type": [
      "http://schema.org/ImageObject"
    ],
    "http://schema.org/url": [
      {
        "@id": "https://medias.mutek.org/artists/_1200x630_crop_center-center_82_none/Élisabeth-Picard.jpg?mtime=1628004812"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "https://montreal.mutek.org/temporary/18236340-850c-4871-8424-34593a70c26f",
    "@type": [
      "http://schema.org/Person"
    ],
    "http://schema.org/image": [
      {
        "@id": "_:g16170600"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/mainEntityOfPage": [
      {
        "@id": "https://montreal.mutek.org/en/artists/elisabeth-picard"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/url": [
      {
        "@id": "https://montreal.mutek.org/en/artists/elisabeth-picard"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasDerivedFrom": [
      {
        "@id": "https://montreal.mutek.org/en/artists/elisabeth-picard"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/description": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Élisabeth Picard  CA/QC — Torsion 01 et Torsion 02  Torsion 01 was inspired by Newton ’s third law; motors are placed at each end of a linear element, twisting on itself, creating the appearance of a floating being. Torsion 02 imitates the helical motion of a storm, complete with electric flashes resembling lightning; the coiling of the element, combined with the violent light flashes, reveals the amount of force exerted on the textile material whose fibers stretch, knot and relax in a continuous motion. Colored strings ranging from dark to fluorescent react to the light, seemingly appearing out of the darkness; vivid yet low-spectrum lights add an intriguing, almost troubling atmosphere.Technical collaboration: Ghislain Brodeur and Matthieu Gagnon Lamarre ."
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/name": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Élisabeth Picard"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
